.TH TORSHIM 1 "September 2026" "torshim 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
torshim \- lightweight launcher for the Tor network (unofficial)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B torshim run
.RI [ options ] " -- " app " [" args ...]
.br
.B torshim
.I app
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B torshim shell
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B sudo torshim connect
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B torshim disconnect
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B torshim repair
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B torshim status
.RB [ --json ] " [" -v ]
.br
.B torshim version
.RB [ -v ]
.br
.B torshim newnym
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B torshim doctor
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
torshim automates the existing
.B tor
daemon: private instance lifecycle, readiness gating, per-app routing,
and isolated child shells. It never implements Tor itself.
Every command fails closed: when Tor is not ready or a route would
leak, torshim exits non-zero instead of claiming protection.
.PP
Unofficial frontend \- not sponsored by The Tor Project. "Tor" is a
registered mark of The Tor Project.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run -- app [args...]
Run one application through Tor. The bare form
.B torshim app [args...]
means the same. On Linux this uses the torsocks LD_PRELOAD shim
(fail-closed: static binaries, non-ELF files, and setuid/setgid
targets are refused). On macOS and Windows it uses proxy environment
(socks5h); only applications honoring proxy env are covered.
.PP
Supervision splits by target. CLI tools take the wait path: the
wrapper blocks until the child exits, forwards SIGINT/SIGTERM to the
child process group, and passes the exit code through. GUI browsers
(firefox, falkon, chromium, chrome) are long-lived and must take the
detach path
.B (run --detach -- browser):
the child is released into a new session with stdio detached
(--log-file PATH captures it, otherwise it is discarded), a bounded
alive poll rejects fast failures, and the parent prints the PID plus
endpoints and exits 0. A bare GUI run is refused with detach guidance
(exit 2) instead of hanging; headless browser runs (--headless,
--screenshot, --dump-dom) stay on the wait path. Detached GUI launches
on macOS/Windows additionally require --acknowledge-gui-risks, an
explicit opt-in to the partial proxy coverage (GPU/D-Bus/single-instance
IPC bypass Tor there).
.TP
.B shell
Spawn a child shell routed through Tor; the parent shell is
untouched. Exit the shell to drop Tor routing. A coverage banner is
printed on entry.
.TP
.B connect (Linux only, needs sudo)
Route the whole system through Tor via iptables/nft transparent
proxy. Snapshots firewall state first, gates on a verify suite, and
rolls back on any failure. Refuses honestly on macOS/Windows.
.TP
.B disconnect
Stop system tor first, restore the firewall byte-exact, post-verify.
Needs sudo when a session exists. Idempotent.
.TP
.B repair
Clear stale rules and dead session records (needs sudo). Never
flushes foreign rules.
.TP
.B status [--json] [-v]
Report tor state. Never claims protected when not. With -v, append
control diagnostics (version, circuits, traffic, listeners).
.TP
.B version [-v]
Print wrapper, platform, per-app mechanism, system-wide support, tor,
and torsocks versions. With -v, add live control-protocol fields when
a tor answers.
.TP
.B newnym
Rotate circuits without restarting tor (SIGNAL NEWNYM against a
persistent tor: --control, TORSHIM_CONTROL session export, system
session, then 9051/9151). Refuses on unready tor (exit 3); tor's own
rate limit surfaces honestly (exit 1, wait spelled out).
.TP
.B doctor
Full health verdict: bootstrap, circuit, SOCKS, DNSPort, exit-IP
egress, firewall, IPv6 (pass/fail/skip rows, exit 0 healthy, 3 not).
Options: --json, -v, --control, --cookie, --socks, --state-dir,
--skip-exit-ip, --skip-dns.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--tor " BIN
tor executable (default: tor).
.TP
.BI "--timeout " D
bootstrap wait budget (default: 120s).
.TP
.B --reuse
Reuse a verifiable foreign tor (cookie auth + readiness gate)
instead of launching a private instance.
.TP
.BI "--backend " "auto|iptables|nft"
connect firewall backend (default: auto).
.TP
.BI "--tor-user " USER
unprivileged user for the system tor.
.TP
.B --force
connect: repair a stale session, then connect.
.TP
.BR -v ", " --verbose
Show the session behind the command: endpoints, mode, circuit,
backend, notices tail (run/shell); diagnostics (status/version);
session ports (connect) or session dir (disconnect/repair); endpoint
resolution (doctor); pre-rotation diagnostics (newnym). To stderr,
never changes the exit code.
.TP
.B --detach
run: GUI path. Release the child into a new session, return the
prompt at once with its PID. Refused children (fast failures) report
their exit instead of a PID.
.TP
.B --wait
run: CLI path. Block until the child exits (the default); mutually
exclusive with --detach.
.TP
.BI "--log-file " PATH
run with --detach: detached child stdout/stderr target (default:
discarded). Rejected without --detach.
.TP
.B --acknowledge-gui-risks
run with --detach on macOS/Windows: accept the partial proxy coverage
for GUI apps (required there, ignored on Linux).
.TP
.BI "--control " ADDR
newnym/doctor/status: control endpoint (default: TORSHIM_CONTROL
session export, system session record, then 9051/9151).
.TP
.BI "--cookie " PATH
newnym/doctor/status: control cookie (default: TOR_COOKIE,
TORSHIM_COOKIE, system session cookie, then conventional paths).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success.
.TP
.B 1
runtime or application failure.
.TP
.B 2
usage error.
.TP
.B 3
tor not ready (fail-closed): bootstrap gated commands, newnym on an
unready tor, and an unhealthy doctor verdict.
.TP
.B 4
feature not available on this platform (system-wide routing on
macOS/Windows: no tun2socks backend shipped; per-app and shell work).
.SH LIMITATIONS
Linux per-app needs the torsocks package. macOS per-app uses proxy
env (no DYLD shim: SIP would strip it silently). Windows per-app uses
proxy env. System-wide connect is Linux-only; macOS would need a
utun+tun2socks+pf path and Windows a wintun+tun2socks+WFP path (not
implemented; the commands refuse honestly with exit 4).
While connected, non-DNS UDP, ICMP, and IPv6 are blocked by design.
Concurrent connect/disconnect/repair runs serialize on a session lock;
a stale lock from a crashed run is reaped, a live one fails closed.
See tor-cli/docs/limitations.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR torsocks (1)
.SH AUTHOR
The Random lab Builder.
